5.1 Data Hub (upload, validation, cleaning)
Drag-and-drop and file picker for .csv, .xlsx, .xls, .json, .tsv, .txt, .xml, .parquet. Multi-sheet Excel: let the user pick a sheet. Show a preview of the first 50 rows, row and column counts, and detected types.
Persistence rule: uploaded data is saved to IndexedDB and remains available across sessions until the user adds new data. On new upload, offer three choices: Replace the dataset, Append (merge by Customer ID with a chosen conflict rule: keep newest, keep existing, or keep both flagged), or Save as a new version. Keep a version history (name, date, rows, quality score) with the ability to restore or delete a version. Never delete data silently, and always confirm destructive actions. Retrain models automatically or on request after new data is added, and flag existing scores as stale.
Validation report (downloadable): required columns present, type mismatches, duplicate Customer IDs, missing values per column (count and %), out-of-range values (Age outside 16–110, Satisfaction outside 1–10, negative charges, Discount outside 0–100, Usage far above Allowance as a warning), date logic (Join Date after Contract Start, Contract End before Start, Churn Date before Join Date, Churn Date present for Active customers), consistency checks (Churn Flag versus Churn Status, Tenure Months versus dates, Age versus Age Band, Direct Debit versus Payment Method), and outliers (IQR or z-score). Produce a 0–100 Data Quality Score with severity tiers (Error, Warning, Info).
Automatic cleaning pipeline (every step logged and reversible, with a before/after summary):
Trim whitespace, standardise casing and category spellings (merge “direct debit”/”Direct Debit”), standardise Yes/No variants (Y, 1, True), parse dates in UK format dd/mm/yyyy as well as ISO.
Remove exact duplicates, resolve duplicate IDs by keeping the most complete or most recent record.
Missing values: numeric median (grouped by Customer Segment where sensible), categorical mode or “Unknown”, structurally blank fields (Churn Date and Reason for active customers) are left blank and not imputed. Add missingness indicator flags for high-missing columns.
Winsorise extreme outliers at the 1st and 99th percentiles (configurable) and record what was capped.
Recompute derived fields where inconsistent (Tenure Months from dates, Age Band from Age).
Let users review, override, or disable any step.
5.2 Feature engineering (automatic)

Create and document these features (show a feature catalogue with definitions):

Usage_to_Allowance_Ratio (usage ÷ allowance, safe division), Overage_Flag, Overage_Share_of_Bill
Effective_Monthly_Charge (charge after discount), Discount_Dependence
Months_to_Contract_End (relative to a configurable “as of” date, defaulting to the latest date in the data), Contract_Expiring_90d, Out_of_Contract_Flag
Payment_Risk_Score (late + failed payments, weighted), Payment_Failure_Rate
Service_Friction_Index (tickets, complaints, resolution hours, outages, standardised and combined), Complaint_Rate_per_Ticket, Slow_Resolution_Flag
Satisfaction_Band, NPS_Category (Detractor 0–6, Passive 7–8, Promoter 9–10 if scale is 0–10; infer the scale from the data and state the assumption), Sat_NPS_Gap
Tenure_Band, Lifecycle_Stage, Price_per_GB
Competitor_Pressure interactions (competitor offer × low satisfaction, competitor offer × contract expiring)
Encoding: one-hot for low-cardinality categories, target-safe frequency or smoothed target encoding for high-cardinality (City, Plan Name) computed inside cross-validation folds to avoid leakage. Scale numeric features for linear models.
5.3 Model Lab (training and evaluation)
Models: Logistic Regression (L2), Random Forest, Gradient Boosting. Optional: a simple baseline (majority class) shown for comparison.
Split and validation: stratified train/validation/test split (default 70/15/15, user-adjustable) plus stratified 5-fold cross-validation on the training set. Set a random seed for reproducibility and record it. Where the dataset has dates, offer an optional time-based split.
Class imbalance: detect it and apply class weights or SMOTE-style oversampling on the training folds only, never on validation or test data.
Tuning: a light grid or random search with a visible progress bar and a cancel button.
Metrics (computed on the held-out test set, with CV mean ± SD): ROC-AUC, PR-AUC, accuracy, precision, recall, F1, balanced accuracy, Brier score, log loss, and the confusion matrix. Verify every metric against a hand-checked formula in unit tests.
Charts: ROC curve, precision-recall curve, confusion matrix heatmap (navy scale, labelled cells), calibration plot, lift and cumulative gains chart, and a model comparison bar chart.
Threshold tuning: an interactive slider showing precision, recall, F1, and expected profit (inputs: average retention offer cost, save rate, monthly charge, assumed remaining lifetime). Recommend a threshold and let the user lock it.
Automatically pick the champion model by a user-selected criterion (default PR-AUC), save it with its feature list, threshold, training date, and metrics, and keep a model registry with versions. Display a plain-English model summary and flag overfitting (train versus test gap), small samples, and low-quality data.
Export the model summary as a PDF and the model’s parameters as JSON.
